    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** stands as our premier offensive anchor with a standout 87.14 score. Facing Zach Agnos, **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a high-upside matchup against a pitcher with a 5.15 xERA, and his projected spot in the two-hole provides the optimal environment for his elite speed to generate the steals we desperately need. **James Wood** also earns a strong recommendation (64.53) despite the presence of a capable catcher behind the plate; his leadoff role against David Peterson carries immense weight, and his impressive 0.381 xwOBA suggests he is primed to exploit this matchup. **Gabriel Moreno** and **Mickey Moniak** round out our high-floor starters, both hovering in the low-60s as they look to capitalize on favorable matchups against suspect rotations.\n\nThe most difficult decision of the morning involved **Tyler Soderstrom** (59.99), who narrowly held his spot against the baseline floor. While he projects as a strong start, the uncertainty regarding his lineup position necessitates a watchful eye; if he drops out of the middle of the order, his utility value will dip significantly. Conversely, **Austin Riley** remains in the lineup despite a sub-optimal BvP history against Sandy Alcantara, as his high base projection keeps him above the marginal threshold. We are monitoring **Henry Bolte** closely, as his projected #8 spot limits his offensive ceiling, though he remains active for now as we prioritize game volume across the board. \n\nWe are currently navigating a depleted bench as we wait for the return of key contributors like **Jose Caballero** and **Giancarlo Stanton**, both of whom are sidelined on the IL. With **Josh Naylor** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** off today, our depth is stretched thin, forcing us to lean on high-variance options like **Kyle Karros** and **Travis Bazzana**. We remain vigilant for any late-breaking lineup changes, as the \"Lineup Pending\" status for many of our players\u2014particularly **Gabriel Moreno**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Austin Riley**\u2014introduces an element of risk regarding their final order placement and the subsequent impact on their efficiency scores.",
